1
00:00:02,276 --> 00:00:04,488
So what's the time complexity of binary

2
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:06,765
search then? Again, we have our different

3
00:00:06,765 --> 00:00:09,282
cases, and the best case is that the item

4
00:00:09,282 --> 00:00:10,917
we're looking for is right in the middle—

5
00:00:11,478 --> 00:00:13,305
not in the start, but in the middle— because

6
00:00:13,338 --> 00:00:14,957
the middle is where we first have a look at.

7
00:00:15,886 --> 00:00:17,522
In our example here, that would be the case

8
00:00:17,522 --> 00:00:20,039
if I'm looking for 9. If I'm looking for 9,

9
00:00:20,071 --> 00:00:21,882
that's the first element we're checking, as

10
00:00:21,914 --> 00:00:22,251
you learned.

11
00:00:22,796 --> 00:00:24,784
And therefore, if I again log this real

12
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:25,313
quick here

13
00:00:26,484 --> 00:00:28,765
you will see that we only see running once

14
00:00:29,118 --> 00:00:31,510
because immediately we find the element.

15
00:00:32,923 --> 00:00:34,722
So that's the best case. The element we're

16
00:00:34,738 --> 00:00:37,436
looking for is right in the middle of our

17
00:00:37,564 --> 00:00:37,805
array.

18
00:00:39,170 --> 00:00:41,883
In this case, we have O, constant time,

19
00:00:41,899 --> 00:00:43,682
because we only need one iteration.

20
00:00:44,388 --> 00:00:46,299
But again, just as with the linear search,

21
00:00:46,331 --> 00:00:48,467
we of course don't know this in advance. We

22
00:00:48,628 --> 00:00:50,506
also might have the worst case where the

23
00:00:50,715 --> 00:00:52,481
item we're looking for is exactly at the

24
00:00:52,497 --> 00:00:54,504
beginning or the end. Since we split the

25
00:00:54,504 --> 00:00:56,108
list in half every time,

26
00:00:56,733 --> 00:00:58,434
we're always having a look at the middle

27
00:00:58,434 --> 00:01:01,257
first. So we only reach the end or the

28
00:01:01,385 --> 00:01:03,920
beginning of the list at the very end of all

29
00:01:04,112 --> 00:01:04,979
our iterations.

30
00:01:05,733 --> 00:01:07,465
And in this case, of course, we don't have

31
00:01:07,481 --> 00:01:09,021
constant time complexity.

32
00:01:09,583 --> 00:01:11,716
But which time complexity do we have?

33
00:01:12,775 --> 00:01:13,224
O?

34
00:01:14,138 --> 00:01:14,828
Actually not.

35
00:01:15,502 --> 00:01:17,635
Actually, we would have logarithmic time

36
00:01:18,245 --> 00:01:22,207
because we split our problem set, our array,

37
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:25,832
in half with every iteration. So no matter

38
00:01:25,848 --> 00:01:28,269
how big our n is, no matter how long our

39
00:01:28,269 --> 00:01:30,386
array is, we're never going to go through

40
00:01:30,658 --> 00:01:32,679
all array items. That's why we don't have

41
00:01:32,679 --> 00:01:34,891
linear time complexity. We're splitting the

42
00:01:34,907 --> 00:01:37,681
array in half every time, and that gives us

43
00:01:37,809 --> 00:01:39,573
logarithmic time complexity.

44
00:01:40,615 --> 00:01:43,566
You might remember this power of algorithm

45
00:01:43,646 --> 00:01:46,596
we had in the math section. There we also

46
00:01:46,676 --> 00:01:48,600
had logarithmic time because what we did

47
00:01:48,696 --> 00:01:51,422
there is also that we divided the number by

48
00:01:51,422 --> 00:01:54,133
2 in every iteration. So we never would run

49
00:01:54,310 --> 00:01:56,751
through all the numbers up to that number.

50
00:01:57,216 --> 00:01:59,368
We always split it in 2, and that means we

51
00:01:59,866 --> 00:02:02,788
have a much smaller problem set. And with

52
00:02:02,916 --> 00:02:05,502
every iteration, we split it in 2, not just

53
00:02:05,630 --> 00:02:08,055
once, which would then be n divided by 2 as

54
00:02:08,055 --> 00:02:09,821
a time complexity or something like that,

55
00:02:10,142 --> 00:02:12,277
but with every iteration. So with every

56
00:02:12,326 --> 00:02:14,959
iteration, it gets smaller and smaller. And

57
00:02:14,959 --> 00:02:15,794
it's the same here

58
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:18,940
since we have a new middle index for every

59
00:02:18,972 --> 00:02:21,586
iteration and we start at that middle index

60
00:02:21,618 --> 00:02:22,067
in the end,

61
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:25,514
we split our array into 2 with every

62
00:02:25,562 --> 00:02:27,069
iteration and derive a new array.

63
00:02:28,031 --> 00:02:30,965
That's why we have O here as the time

64
00:02:30,997 --> 00:02:31,639
complexity.

65
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:34,749
The average case is that we'll have a sorted

66
00:02:34,749 --> 00:02:36,833
list but we don't know where the item is and

67
00:02:36,849 --> 00:02:38,372
that will be our default case.

68
00:02:39,094 --> 00:02:41,290
And just as before with the linear search,

69
00:02:41,723 --> 00:02:44,273
this here also therefore tends to be our

70
00:02:44,305 --> 00:02:46,774
worst case. We might be better than that but

71
00:02:46,774 --> 00:02:49,419
we don't know. So in the end, we assume we

72
00:02:49,419 --> 00:02:51,182
have the worst case, we could say.

73
00:02:51,759 --> 00:02:52,079
So

74
00:02:52,624 --> 00:02:54,692
often we'll be a bit better, sometimes we'll

75
00:02:54,724 --> 00:02:57,385
be exactly at the worst case, rarely we'll

76
00:02:57,529 --> 00:03:00,014
be in the best case. Therefore, our time

77
00:03:00,014 --> 00:03:02,626
complexity here would also be O.

78
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:05,575
That are the time complexities here.

79
00:03:06,169 --> 00:03:08,364
And that's a general good rule of thumb. If

80
00:03:08,413 --> 00:03:11,538
you're splitting the problem set into 2 or

81
00:03:11,538 --> 00:03:14,808
even smaller, divided by 3 or 4, with every

82
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:17,458
iteration, you're dealing with logarithmic

83
00:03:17,523 --> 00:03:17,850
time.
